// c57: `extern "C"` foreign imports (ADR 0057) — the FIRST fixture in `tests/` to
// use `extern` at all. That absence was not cosmetic: the whole fixture-driven
// differential corpus (lex, parse, resolve, types, borrow, effects, mir, codegen)
// is built from `tests/pass` + `tests/ui`, so every stage's extern handling was
// structurally untestable, and two real defects sat green because of it — the
// self-hosted `scg` registered no extern declarations at all (emitting NO CALL
// where the oracle emits one), and its merge silently deleted whole programs that
// used the ADR 0057 A9 `link(...)` clause. ADR 0057's own phasing anticipated this,
// deferring the `scg` mirror "until a corpus fixture uses `extern`".
//
// The fixture is deliberately shaped to guard the ORDERING invariant, which is the
// part that is easy to get wrong and expensive to get wrong. The Rust resolver
// registers every own `fn` FIRST and every `extern "C"` declaration AFTER, so here
// `wrap` is FnId 42, `main` 43, and `llabs` 44 — even though the extern block comes
// FIRST in the source (verified against `snc resolve`, which prints
// `(fn #42 wrap … (call #44 …))`). Registering in source order — the natural mistake
// — would make `llabs` 42 and shift every other id.
//
// Which differentials actually catch what, stated precisely rather than optimistically:
//   * an ID SHIFT surfaces in FOUR of the nine — resolve, types, borrow, effects.
//     `mir` and `llvm` render callees by NAME, and lex/ast run before resolution, so
//     those four are the whole guard.
//   * dropping extern REGISTRATION entirely surfaces more widely: resolve/types emit
//     `(call #)` with no id, and `llvm` loses both the `declare` and the call.
//   * dropping the PARSER's extern arm surfaces in ast (malformed, unbalanced parens);
//     dropping the LEXER keyword surfaces in lex (`Ident` for `Extern`).
// Note the asymmetry: only the resolve differential covers `selfhost/resolve`, so that
// stage has exactly one guard here, not four.
//
// `llabs` is chosen for portability, not flavour: it is C99, lives in libc on
// macOS/Linux AND in the MSVC CRT on Windows, and is genuinely `i64 -> i64` (unlike
// `abs`/`labs`, which are `int`/`long` and so mismatch the declared ABI on some
// platforms). `getpid`, used by `examples/sys/process_ids.sentinel`, is NOT portable
// this way — it has no MSVC CRT equivalent, which is why that example is a known
// Windows link failure and why it could not serve as the corpus fixture.
//
// FFI values are PUBLIC (the unverified boundary): a `secret` argument crossing an
// extern call is rejected by the ADR 0057 secret fence, so none of this is `secret`.

extern "C" {
    fn llabs(x: i64) -> i64;
}

// Wrapping the call in a non-`main` fn mirrors the real `std::sys::posix` shape,
// where each libc symbol is fronted by a safe Sentinel wrapper.
fn wrap(v: i64) -> i64 {
    llabs(v)
}

fn main() -> i64 {
    // Two call sites, one already-positive, so a stubbed-out `llabs` returning its
    // argument unchanged would still fail the negative case.
    let a: i64 = wrap(0 - 20);
    let b: i64 = wrap(22);
    a + b
}
